(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["interpretation-of-medical-literature-book-questions-chapter-08-prognosis-quiz"]={"title":"Chapter 8: Prognosis Quiz — Book Questions — Interpretation of Medical Literature","category":"Interpretation of Medical Literature","exam":"Book Questions","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"For a study of the risk of esophageal cancer in patients with Barrett esophagus (a precancerous lesion), which of the following times in the course of disease is the best example of zero time?","choices":["Diagnosis of Barrett esophagus for each patient","Death of each patient","Diagnosis of esophageal cancer for each patient","Calendar time when the first patient is enrolled in the study","Calendar time when no patient remains in the study"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Zero time is at the onset of disease (in this case, Barrett esophagus), not the time of outcome events (in this case, esophageal cancer). — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 8.1"},{"q":"A cohort study describes the recurrence of seizure within 1 year in children hospitalized with a first febrile seizure. It compared recurrence in children who had infection versus immunization as an underlying cause for fever at the time of the first seizure. Some of the children were no longer in the study when outcome (a second seizure) was assessed at 1 year. Which of the following would have the greatest effect on study results?","choices":["Why the children dropped out","When in the course of follow-up the children left the study","Whether dropping out was related to prognosis","Whether the number of children dropping out is similar in the groups"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Different rates of dropping out would bias results only if those who dropped out had a systematically different prognosis from those who remained. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 8.2"},{"q":"A cohort study of prostate cancer care compares rates of incontinence in patients who were treated with surgery versus medical care alone. Incontinence is assessed from review of medical records. Which of the following is not an example of measurement bias?","choices":["Men were more likely to tell their surgeon about incontinence.","Surgeons were less likely to record complications of their surgery in the record.","Men who got surgery were more likely to have follow-up visits.","Chart abstractors used their judgment is deciding whether incontinence was present or not.","Rates of incontinence were higher in the men who got surgery."],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Responses A–D are all possible reasons for measurement bias, whereas a true difference in incontinence rates between patients treated with surgery versus medical care would not be a systematic error. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 8.3"},{"q":"A clinical prediction rule has been developed to classify the prognosis of community-acquired pneumonia. Which of the following is most characteristic of such a clinical prediction rule?","choices":["Calculating a score is simple.","The clinical data are readily available.","Multiple prognostic factors are included.","The results are used to guide further management of the patient.","All of the above."],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Responses A–D are all features of clinical prediction rules. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 8.4"},{"q":"A study describes the clinical course of patients who have an uncommon neurologic disease. Patients are identified at a referral center that specializes in this disease. Their medical records are reviewed for patients' characteristics, treatments, and their current disease status. Which of the following best describes this kind of study?","choices":["Cohort study","Case-control study","Case series","Cross-sectional study","A randomized controlled trial"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. are a hybrid research strategy, not describing the clinical course in a cohort nor relative risk with a case-control approach nor studying a representative sample of prevalent cases. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 8.5"},{"q":"A study used time-to-event analysis to describe the survival from diagnosis of 100 patients with congestive heart failure. By the third year, 60 patients have been censored. Which of the following would not be a reason for one of these patients being censored?","choices":["The patient died of another cause before year 3.","The patient decided not to continue in the study.","The patient had been enrolled in the study for less than 3 years.","The patient developed another disease that could be fatal."],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Patients would be censored for any reason that removes them from the study or causes them not to be in the study for as long as 3 years. If the outcome is survival, having another potentially fatal disease would not matter if they had not yet died of it. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 8.6"},{"q":"Which of the following kinds of studies cannot be used to identify prognostic factors?","choices":["Prevalence study","Time-to-event analysis","Case-control study","Cohort study"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Prognosis is about disease outcomes over time, and prevalence studies do not measure events over time. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 8.7"},{"q":"Which of the following best describes the information in a survival curve?","choices":["An unbiased estimate of survival even if some patients leave the study","The estimated probability of survival from zero time","The proportion of a cohort still alive at the end of follow-up","The rate at which original members of the cohort leave the study","The cumulative survival of a cohort over time"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Survival curves estimate survival in a cohort, after taking into account censored patients, and do not directly describe the proportion of the original cohort who survived. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 8.8"},{"q":"Which of the following is the most appropriate sample for a study of prognosis?","choices":["Members of the general population","Patients in primary care in the community","Patients admitted to a community hospital","Patients referred to a specialist","It depends on who will use the results of the study."],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. All of the responses might be correct depending on the research question. For example, it would be useful to know prognosis both among patient in primary care and also among those referred to specialists. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 8.9"},{"q":"Investigators wish to describe the clinical course of multiple sclerosis. They take advantage of a clinical trial, already completed, in which control patients received usual care. Patients in the trial had been identified at referral centers, had been enrolled at the time of diagnosis, and had met rigorous entry criteria. After 10 years, all patients had been examined yearly and remained under observation, and 40% were still able to walk. Which of the following most limits the credibility of this study?","choices":["Inconsistent zero time","Generalizability","Measurement bias","Migration bias","Failure to use time-to-event methods"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Because patients had to meet stringent criteria to be included in the trial, the clinical course of those assigned to usual care would not be representative of patients with the disease, as they occur in the general population or in a defined clinical setting. Therefore, the results would not be generalizable to any naturally occurring group of patients with multiple sclerosis. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 8.10"},{"q":"A cohort study is done to assess how often headaches are present in children who have had concussions. At the scheduled 5-year follow-up, one- fifth of the participants have dropped out of the study. The investigators consider calculating the incidence of headaches using multiple imputation. Which of the following is correct?","choices":["Multiple imputation assumes that the characteristics measured in the study can be used to reliably estimate the missing values.","Multiple imputation removes the effects of bias from incomplete follow-up.","Multiple imputation can estimate headache rates for the missing patients by randomly selecting whether headache is present in patients with complete follow- up.","Complete case analysis compares the results for patients with complete information to results for those with missing values."],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Multiple imputation is a technique to estimate values for missing data. It uses statistical modeling of patients with complete data to estimate the missing values. A simple and likely biased method of imputation would be to take the average of the measured values in patients with complete data as an estimate for the missing value. Multiple imputation takes other characteristics into account to make the estimate. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 8.11"},{"q":"Many different clinical prediction rules have been developed to assess the severity of community-acquired pneumonia. Which of the following is the most important reason for choosing one to use?","choices":["The prediction rule classifies patients into groups with very different prognosis.","The prediction rule has been validated in different settings.","Many variables are included in the rule.","Prognostic factors include state-of-the-science diagnostic tests.","The score is calculated using computers."],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Even if clinical prediction rules are constructed using the best available methods, the strongest test of their ability to predict is that they have been shown to do so in patients other that the ones used to develop the rule. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 8.12"},{"q":"In a study of patients who smoke and developed peripheral arterial disease, the hazard ratio for amputation in patients who continued to smoke, relative to those who quit smoking, is 5. Which of the following best characterizes the hazard ratio?","choices":["In this study, it is the rate of continued smoking divided by the rate of quitting.","It can be estimated from a case-control study of smoking and amputation.","It cannot be adjusted for the presence or absence of other factors related to prognosis.","It conveys information similar to relative risk.","It is calculated from the cumulative incidence of amputation in smokers and quitters."],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A hazard ratio is calculated from information in a survival curve and is similar but not identical to relative risk. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 8.13"},{"q":"In a time-to-event analyses, the event:","choices":["Can occur only once","Is dichotomous","Both A and B","Neither A nor B"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Outcome events in time-to-event analyses are either/or events that occur only once. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 8.14"}]};
